people of the internet retro recontour  here and in today's video we're going to  be talking about the new hit ps5 title  returnal and five ways it's similar to  one of my favorite series of all time  metroid oh dude this actually reminds me  the opening of metroid prime right there  remember metroid prime when it's like  looking up and then it like  it pans down  and then you get the ba  [Music]  now a lot of these comparisons will be  to metroid prime however there are some  to the older 2d metroid games as well  prime may be a first-person shooter or  first-person adventure as nintendo  preferred to call it  while returnal is a third-person  roguelike but there are still numerous  elements along the way that make it  pretty clear to me how smart took at  least some  inspiration from the metroid series  number one  the atmosphere for both games it really  starts with the atmosphere  this struck me almost immediately when i  booted up paterno for the first  time sure there are plenty of games that  take place in space but not all of them  focus on the quiet mystery of space  begging to be explored from the moment  celine's ship crashes onto an unfamiliar  planet you're met by a dark yet lush  environment with rain falling  overhead mysterious vegetation and  ancient runes waiting to be deciphered  it really reminded me of the early  scenes in metroid prime when samus lands  on talon 4 for the first time  also in the rain met with plants and  vegetation  as well as various artifacts to be  analyzed both games nail that strange  sense of being  isolated out in space yet surrounded by  the unfamiliar at the same time  the environments are packed with detail  encouraging you to uncover all their  secrets  i also can't say enough about the slick  menu system and hud in each game  prime may have felt a little more  immersive because of its first person  perspective which gave retro studios the  chance to have the game's menu appear as  part of samus's visor which worked  incredibly well even though celine's  adventure is third person the menu is  also incredibly slick  futuristic and perhaps most important of  all informative  number two scanning and item discovery  to learn more about the planets in each  game you'll have the ability to scan  objects and artifacts along the way  in prime you have the ability to switch  to your scan visor at any time and look  over the world for scannable objects  returnal has opted instead for a  contextual approach where prompts will  appear on screen when you're near an  item that can be scanned  to this day prime is a game that  receives near unanimous praise yet one  of the few qualms some like to nitpick  is the scanning and maybe the  overuse of said scanning with returnal  keeping the scanning only as needed it's  possible it will avoid this criticism  i for one enjoy it in both games it  feels like a very natural way for  someone exploring an unfamiliar planet  to learn more about the world  the in-game menus in both games also do  an excellent job of displaying and  categorizing this information in case  you need to reference it again  later on number three weapon and suit  upgrades  this right here has been a staple of  metroid games  since the original you lose all your  abilities and then you slowly  gather them back including new abilities  throughout the game  from missiles to a dash ability to the  morph ball to morph ball bombs  samus has quite the arsenal to take on  space pirates  with house mark's pedigree it should  come as no surprise that the weapons in  returnal are  incredible like metroid you'll gather  upgrades along the way that enhance your  abilities  including allowing you to reach areas  that you couldn't before  to explore the world you'll need both  weapon and suit upgrades  and whereas metroid's power-ups are  pretty clear-cut  returnal instead has power-ups that can  actually hinder you  while they help you it's a careful  trade-off and one that i'm really  enjoying so  far in return number four female  heroines  that feel similar back in 1986 with the  original metroid nintendo surprised the  gaming world with a series of secret  endings that revealed the game's  protagonist  was actually female now here in 2021  this may not seem like a big deal as  more and more games feature female leads  these days but back then this was quite  a surprise to many  samus remains a gaming icon even today  no thanks to nintendo might i add  seriously  just give us the prime collection  already so here we are in 2021 and it's  not a huge surprise to have celine as  the main protagonist for eternal but  credit to games like metroid tomb raider  perfect dark and plenty of others that  have helped pave the way but it's not  just the fact that they're women with  blonde hair in a spacesuit  celine also controls similar to how  samus moves in the 2d titles like super  metroid and metroid fusion you'll notice  right away that she's fast and has a  dash ability to help her cross long gaps  it certainly tends to lean more to the  arcadey side of things than the  realistic side of things  and as far as this specific aspect goes  this is actually different from the  prime games which moved more like a  traditional fps  except for maybe when samus was in the  morph ball mode at number five we have  world navigation both the map and vault  doors in return  look as if they were ripped straight out  of metroid prime and i totally say that  as a compliment now seriously just check  out the map right here this one's from  metroid prime and then compare it to  what we have here  with return all these look so similar  guys and you know nothing wrong with us  i think this is great inspiration from  house mark looking back at what retro  studios did and it actually works really  really well  in both games now any metroid fan is  gonna know a series staple is  backtracking going through areas that  you've already seen once  but now you have a special ability or a  special item that you didn't have before  that's going to allow you into an area  you couldn't reach  now i'm still early in return but i've  already noticed some things like this  now of course the format for eternal is  very different it is a roguelike  with a procedural nature so it's  certainly not that same  exact open world in the sense that you  see in something like metroid prime or  even super metroid  what prime does so well and what eternal  is doing well so far  is the sense of progression what's  interesting about those doors in metroid  prime is retro  actually used it as a clever way to hide  some of the loading screens  in fact there really were no loading  screens whatsoever in metroid prime  unless you count the little elevator  sequence which  again was kind of a clever way for retro  to hide loading to a new area  with playstation 5 super fast ssd it  doesn't seem like housemark is using it  as a way to hide loading but in terms of  the design perspective to me it is  clearly inspired by the doors of metroid  prime you'll even come across  red doors but you need a special key to  access metroid had various types of  doors as well which required you to  switch to a different beam or even  require the missile  now guys i am still in the early hours  of return  so i can't wait to see what else i find  as i play along this is actually my  first return  video so if you guys are enjoying this  and you're also a metroid fan let me  know what similarities you have found  which ones did i miss  which ones did you notice as well and if  you want to see more videos like this  hit that thumbs up before you go  as always thank you all so much for  watching i'll talk to you again soon
 

